### Page 3 of 7

```text
Resolution approving the budget of the Downtown Development Authority for the Fiscal
Year beginning July 1, 2012 and ending June 30, 2013 (postponed from 6/11/12).  John Sundahl,
President, Downtown Development Authority (DDA) Board, advised he was present to answer any
questions, and requested approval of a substitute resolution/budget.  Mr. Brown moved to adopt,
seconded by Mr. Pierson.  Mr. Brown moved to amend by substitute, seconded by Mr. Pierson.  It
was noted the proposed substitute reflects a reduction in the amount of the overall proposed budget
(from $626,220.00 to $621,370.00).  Motion to amend carried.  Voting “yes” -- all members of the
governing body.  Main motion as amended by substitute carried.  Voting “yes” -- all members of
the governing body. (#5403)
Resolution prioritizing the City of Cheyenne’s 2012 applications for Mineral Royalty Capital
Construction Grants. Mr. Brown moved to adopt, seconded by Mr. Valdez.  Mr. Brown moved to
amend by substitute, seconded by Mr. Valdez.  It was advised the substitute clarifies the
prioritization order, which was inadvertently listed in the wrong order on the initial resolution.
Cindy Schneider, Grants Manager, reviewed the order of the prioritization of projects listed in the
proposed substitute as follows:  #1. East Pershing Blvd. Sanitary Sewer (Dunn to Converse
Avenues), #2. Crow Creek Water Reclamation Facility Improvements, #3. Street & Alley Road
Maintenance Facility Addition, Phase I, and #4. Snyder Avenue Sewer Rehabilitation Project (West
24 St. To West Pershing Blvd.).  Motion to amend carried.  Voting “yes” -- all members of the
th
governing body.  Upon inquiry, Tim Wilson, BOPU Director, briefly reviewed the Priority No. 4
project, clarifying it was a continuation of the Snyder Ave. and West Pershing Blvd. reconstruction
project which will include replacement of part of the sewer line.  Main motion as amended by
substitute carried.  Voting “yes” -- all members of the governing body. (#5404)
Resolution authorizing the Mayor and the City Clerk to sign a Final Plat for Lincoln
Addition, Second Filing, a replat of a portion of Tract B, Lincoln Addition, and abandoned WYDOT
right-of-way being situated in a portion of Section 1, T.13N., R.67W., 6th P.M., Cheyenne,
Wyoming (located north of and adjacent to West Lincolnway, between Westland Road and Stinson
Avenue).  Ms. Ash  moved to adopt with staff recommendation #1, seconded by Dr. Rinne.  Upon
inquiry, Brett Walker, Development Office, stated the staff recommendation requires the applicant
to submit a revised traffic impact study, which has already been done and is under review by the
Engineer's office.  Motion carried.  Voting "yes" -- all members of the governing body. (#5405)
Resolution re-appropriating the Fiscal Year 2012 budget of the City of Cheyenne according
to the provisions of W.S. §16-4-112 and 16-4-113.  Mr. Brown moved to adopt, seconded by Mr.
Spiker.  Mr. Brown moved to amend by substitute, seconded by Mr. Spiker.  It was advised the
amendment increases the employee health insurance share program total payment amount from
$165,000.00 to $181,000.00.  Upon inquiry, Lois Huff, City Treasurer, advised the substitute was
prepared based upon comments made at Finance Committee and explained the program which
involves full-time employees during calendar year 2011, but not elected officials; health insurance
savings during calendar year 2011 due to employee health wellness efforts to keep down premium
costs, and eligibility provisions to receive a portion of the savings.  Ms. Huff also provided
information on employee health insurance savings realized during the budget process last year,
noting $65,000.00 of the amount saved is going toward the Employee Investment Study, and stated,
if the substitute is approved, there will be approximately $19,000 in savings remaining in the line
item.  It was clarified elected officials will not receive any payment, and that representatives for the
Police Protective Association, Cheyenne Public Employees Association and the Firefighters local
union were all contacted and in agreement with the proposal.  Motion to amend carried.  Voting
```

